GRAVES = [
{"id":"everest-1996-disaster","name":"The 1996 Everest Disaster","year":1996,"country":"Nepal","industry":"Mountaineering and commercial expeditions","founded":1996,"failed":1996,
"summary":"On 10 May 1996, two commercial expeditions led by celebrated guides Rob Hall and Scott Fischer pushed for the summit of Everest in deteriorating weather, ignoring their own turnaround times. A storm trapped climbers high in the death zone overnight. Eight people died that night, including Rob Hall and Scott Fischer, in the deadliest season Everest had then seen. Clients with vastly different abilities had paid large fees to be guided up a mountain where, above 8,000 metres, no guide can carry you, oxygen runs out by the hour, and the queue at the Hillary Step becomes a traffic jam inside a killing altitude. The disaster was later chronicled in bestselling books and inquiries that still argue about blame. The commercial structure itself is the autopsy's subject: summit fever sold as a product, experience thresholds quietly lowered, fixed ropes and oxygen logistics stretched across too many clients, and turnaround rules that existed precisely for that day and were waived on it. Everest did not become safe afterward. It became more crowded, and the same incentives still sell places in the queue.",
"mistakes":"The first failure was the waived turnaround time, the one rule designed to be obeyed exactly when ambition screams loudest. The second was commercial: clients were sold a summit probability their physiology had not earned, and guides competed on success rates in a market that punishes the cautious. Oxygen and fixed rope logistics were planned for the itinerary, not for the delay the Hillary Step queue imposed. Radios, weather information and unified command between the two big teams were inadequate for a shared emergency. Underneath sat the oldest mountain error in modern dress: the summit had become the product, so descent, where most deaths occur, was budgeted as an afterthought.",
"lessons":"A turnaround rule you waive on the big day is not a rule; it is a decoration. Write it, brief it, and let it cost you the summit in front of witnesses. Sell honest probabilities: any service that guarantees outcomes in lethal environments has already begun lying about the risks. Plan logistics for the queue and the storm, not for the brochure itinerary, because altitude charges by the hour. And remember that in the death zone every client is ultimately self reliant. Systems that pretend otherwise are manufacturing the next memorial.",
"timeline":[
["1996","Commercial guiding on Everest is booming; large teams book overlapping summit windows for 10 May."],
["10 May 1996","Dozens of climbers summit late in the day as weather worsens. Turnaround times pass unenforced."],
["Night of 10 to 11 May","A blizzard strands climbers above 8,000 metres. Eight die, including guides Rob Hall and Scott Fischer."],
["May 1996","Survivors descend over following days. The season's death toll reaches twelve, then the worst on record."],
["1997","Jon Krakauer's Into the Wild era account in Into Thin Air and Anatoly Boukreev's The Climb offer rival versions; the argument shapes mountaineering ethics for decades."],
["2000s onward","Commercial traffic multiplies, with permit queues photographed at the Hillary Step in 2019, renewing the same debate."],
["Today","Everest guiding is a mature industry with better forecasting and oxygen systems, and the 1996 questions, who belongs up there and who may say turn back, remain open."]]},
{"id":"grenfell-tower-2017","name":"Grenfell Tower and the Cladding Economy","year":2017,"country":"United Kingdom","industry":"Housing, construction and regulation","founded":1974,"failed":2017,
"summary":"Grenfell Tower, a 24 storey council housing block in North Kensington, London, was refurbished in 2015 to 2016 with aluminium composite cladding panels containing a polyethylene core, a material that burns like solid petrol, installed over combustible insulation, partly to improve the tower's appearance for its wealthier neighbours. In the early hours of 14 June 2017 a fridge freezer fire in a fourth floor flat escaped through a window and ignited the cladding. The fire raced up the outside of the building, re entered flats through windows, and defeated the stay put policy that assumed each flat was a fireproof box. Seventy two people died. Residents had warned the landlord's management organisation for years, in writing, about fire safety, power surges and blocked exits; their blog predicted a catastrophe would be required before anyone listened. The public inquiry documented a decades long system failure: deregulated building control, manufacturers who gamed fire tests and mis-sold products, cost cutting at every hand off, and a state that treated social housing residents as a nuisance to manage. Prosecution decisions have taken most of a decade. Millions of people elsewhere discovered their own buildings wore similar skins.",
"mistakes":"Every layer of the system sold its responsibility to the next. Manufacturers tested dishonestly and marketed combustible panels for high rise use. Designers and contractors value engineered the safer zinc cladding originally specified into the cheaper combustible system to save a modest sum. Building control, partly privatised and competing for clients, approved what it should never have passed. The tenant management organisation treated complaining residents as adversaries and let fire risk assessments rot. The government ignored coroners' recommendations from a previous tower fire in 2009. The deepest failure is written in the residents' own archive: the people who knew, said so, in writing, for years, and were filed under difficult.",
"lessons":"A resident complaint is a sensor, and a landlord that files sensors under difficult has disabled its own alarm system. Value engineering on life safety components is not savings; it is the purchase of a future inquiry at a discount. Test results from manufacturers must be verified as if they were advertising, because in this case they were. Stay put policies depend entirely on the building actually performing as assumed; strategy cannot outrun materials. And listen for the Grenfell sentence in your own organisation: it will take a catastrophe for anyone to listen. When you hear it, you are already in the timeline.",
"timeline":[
["1974","Grenfell Tower is completed as social housing in North Kensington."],
["2009","The Lakanal House tower fire in London kills six. A coroner later recommends reviewing stay put guidance and external fire spread rules."],
["2012 onward","Grenfell residents report fire safety concerns, power surges and maintenance failures in writing and on a public blog."],
["2015 to 2016","Refurbishment wraps the tower in aluminium composite cladding with a flammable polyethylene core over combustible insulation."],
["14 Jun 2017","A flat fire ignites the cladding shortly before 1 a.m. The tower is consumed within hours. Seventy two people die."],
["2017 to 2024","The Grenfell Tower Inquiry documents systemic failures in product testing, regulation, procurement and resident treatment."],
["2020 onward","A building safety crisis unfolds as thousands of blocks are found with similar cladding; leaseholders face years of costs and limbo."],
["Today","A new Building Safety Act and regulator exist. Criminal charging decisions remain pending years after the fire, itself a second lesson in institutional speed."]]},
{"id":"rana-plaza-2013","name":"Rana Plaza: The Price Sewn Into the Label","year":2013,"country":"Bangladesh","industry":"Garment manufacturing and global supply chains","founded":2013,"failed":2013,
"summary":"Rana Plaza in Savar, outside Dhaka, was an eight storey building designed for shops and offices and converted, floor by illegally added floor, into garment factories housing thousands of machines and workers producing for European and American brands. On 23 April 2013 large cracks appeared in the structure; the building was evacuated and a bank and shops below closed. Factory owners, under delivery pressure, ordered workers back in the next morning, some reportedly with threats to wages. At about 8:45 a.m. on 24 April the building collapsed. At least 1,134 people were killed and some 2,500 injured, the deadliest disaster in garment industry history. Rescue ran for weeks through concrete that had been poured for retail loads and loaded with industrial generators. The collapse detonated the industry's deniability: labels of famous brands were pulled from the rubble, and within weeks over 200 companies signed the Bangladesh Accord, a legally binding safety agreement with inspections and remediation, while a rival alliance of American brands chose a weaker voluntary scheme. A decade on, the Accord model expanded to Pakistan, wages and safety remain contested, and the price pressure that built Rana Plaza still flows down every purchase order.",
"mistakes":"The building was a fraud in concrete, permitted for one use, extended illegally, and loaded beyond design, while inspectors were bribed or absent. Factory owners then made the moral decision inside the structural one: production targets over an evacuated building, workers' fear priced lower than late delivery penalties. The brands above them had engineered the blindness they enjoyed, auditing paperwork through layers of subcontracting while squeezing price and lead time in ways that made safety a fiction everyone signed. The state's regulators were outnumbered and compromised. Each layer truthfully claimed the collapse was not on its desk, which is how 1,134 people ended up on nobody's.",
"lessons":"Your supply chain's real standards are set by your price and deadline, not your code of conduct; audit what your purchase order makes possible. Binding beats voluntary when lives are priced against margins: the Accord's legal teeth exist because Rana Plaza proved signatures without them are stationery. Workers need a refusal channel that survives payday: the right to decline an unsafe building, protected and paid, is the cheapest inspection regime ever designed. And subcontracting depth is a moral choice. If you do not know which building your product is in, you have decided not to know.",
"timeline":[
["2006 onward","Rana Plaza rises for commercial use, then gains extra floors and heavy garment machinery without proper permission or structure."],
["23 Apr 2013","Cracks appear. The building is evacuated; an engineer is reported to declare it unsafe. Shops and a bank close."],
["24 Apr 2013","Factory workers are sent back to their machines in the morning. At about 8:45 a.m. the building collapses."],
["Apr to May 2013","Rescue and recovery run for 19 days. The toll reaches at least 1,134 dead and about 2,500 injured."],
["May 2013","The Accord on Fire and Building Safety in Bangladesh is signed by global brands and unions: binding inspections, remediation and worker participation."],
["2013 onward","Owner Sohel Rana and officials face prosecution; compensation funds for victims become their own long struggle."],
["2021","The Accord renews as the International Accord and later extends to Pakistan."],
["Today","Bangladesh factories are measurably safer structurally, while wages, union rights and price pressure remain the unfinished half of the autopsy."]]},
{"id":"triangle-shirtwaist-1911","name":"The Triangle Shirtwaist Fire","year":1911,"country":"United States","industry":"Garment industry and workplace safety","founded":1901,"failed":1911,
"summary":"On 25 March 1911, near closing time on a Saturday, fire broke out on the eighth floor of the Asch Building in New York, home of the Triangle Waist Company, employing some 500 workers, mostly young immigrant women, making shirtwaist blouses. The factory floors were crowded with flammable cloth, cuttings littered the floor, smoking was common despite rules, and exit doors opened inward or were kept locked to prevent theft and unauthorised breaks. The fire escapes collapsed under fleeing workers; elevators stopped; the hose on the ninth floor found no water pressure to matter. One hundred and forty six workers died in eighteen minutes, many leaping from windows before crowds of helpless onlookers because the ladders of the fire department reached only to the sixth floor. Owners Blanck and Harris were tried for manslaughter and acquitted, later settling civil claims for a fraction per life. The outrage built modern workplace safety: New York's Factory Investigating Commission, led by Al Smith and Robert Wagner and staffed by a young Frances Perkins, drove dozens of laws on exits, sprinklers, drills and inspections that became the national template. Frances Perkins, watching the fire, later built the New Deal's labour protections.",
"mistakes":"Every element of the deaths was a management decision wearing the costume of an accident. Doors locked against theft valued blouses over the women sewing them; the owners had insured the stock far more carefully than the staff. Fire drills were resisted as lost production, escapes went unmaintained, and the building's legal compliance was itself compliant with a code that had been written for property, not people. The owners had also recently broken a strike demanding, among other things, safer conditions, so the disaster arrived pre argued: the workers had itemised their own deaths as grievances and been refused. The acquittal then demonstrated that criminal law, like the building code, had not yet been designed to see them.",
"lessons":"If your exits are locked for security, you have published your true valuation of the people inside. Safety demands made by workers are inspection reports delivered free; refusing them is recorded, eventually, by other means. Insurance is not safety: the Triangle owners recovered more for the building than the families did for the dead, an arithmetic the law has spent a century correcting. And reform follows atrocity unless institutions learn to move on evidence instead of funerals; Frances Perkins spent a career trying to make her country legislate before the fire, not only after it.",
"timeline":[
["1909","The Uprising of the 20,000: shirtwaist workers strike for union recognition and safer conditions. Triangle settles wages but refuses the union and key safety demands."],
["25 Mar 1911","Fire erupts at Triangle around 4:40 p.m. One hundred and forty six workers die in about eighteen minutes."],
["Apr 1911","Some 400,000 New Yorkers march or watch the funeral procession in the rain. Public outrage becomes political organisation."],
["Jun 1911","New York creates the Factory Investigating Commission, with Frances Perkins among its driving investigators."],
["Dec 1911","Owners Blanck and Harris are acquitted of manslaughter. Civil settlements later average about 75 dollars per victim's family."],
["1911 to 1914","New York enacts dozens of safety laws: exits, sprinklers, drills, occupancy and inspection standards that spread nationally."],
["1913","Blanck is fined 20 dollars for again locking a factory door during working hours."],
["Today","The Asch Building survives as an NYU building with plaques. The victims' names were all finally identified and publicly read only in 2011, a century later."]]},
{"id":"dust-bowl-1930s","name":"The Dust Bowl: Farming the Sky Away","year":1935,"country":"United States","industry":"Agriculture and ecology","founded":1930,"failed":1935,
"summary":"In the 1930s the southern Great Plains of the United States, ploughed up in a wheat boom and then struck by drought, began to blow away. Decades of deep ploughing, encouraged by war prices, tractor credit and real estate promotion, had destroyed the native grassland whose roots had held the soil through dry centuries. When drought came, there was nothing left to hold it. Black blizzards of topsoil buried fences, houses and lungs; on Black Sunday, 14 April 1935, a single storm turned noon to midnight across several states, and dust from the plains fell on Washington, D.C. while Congress debated soil conservation, the era's most effective lobbying, delivered by the sky itself. Some 2.5 million people left the plains states in the decade, the largest migration in American history, into a California that did not want them. Hundreds of thousands of farms failed. The federal response created the Soil Conservation Service, taught contour ploughing and strip farming, planted shelterbelts of trees, and bought back submarginal land, stabilising much of the region by the 1940s, with help from returning rain. The autopsy is of a feedback loop: incentives paid men to plough the protection off the land, and the bill arrived addressed to their children.",
"mistakes":"The economics paid for the catastrophe in advance: homestead laws sized for humid farming, wheat prices, and land speculation all rewarded breaking sod that should never have been broken, and punished the restraint that looked like laziness to neighbours. Mechanisation multiplied the exposure, one tractor erasing in a season the work of grass over centuries. Science and government promoted settlement with rainfall theories that told farmers what they wanted to hear. When drought came, tenancy and debt forced continued cropping of dying land, because the bank's calendar outranks the soil's. No villain twirled a moustache; the ledger itself was the villain, and it paid quarterly.",
"lessons":"Incentives are environmental policy whether or not they mention the environment: subsidise the ploughing and you will get the dust. Every landscape has a use it cannot survive, and the people farming it are usually the last who can afford to stop. Conservation arrived when the victims' topsoil physically entered the legislature: feedback must be made that tangible, that fast, by measurement and storytelling, before a system corrects. And beware development theories that flatter settlement. The rain did not follow the plough; it simply left, as it periodically does, to audit what the plough had done.",
"timeline":[
["1900s to 1920s","Mechanisation, war demand and land promotion drive mass ploughing of native grassland across the southern plains."],
["1931","Drought begins. Wheat prices collapse with the Depression, farmers plough more to chase income from dying land."],
["1932 to 1934","Dust storms multiply. 1934 brings one of the worst years; soil blows to the East Coast and onto ships at sea."],
["14 Apr 1935","Black Sunday: a colossal duster darkens the plains at midday. Dust reaches Washington during soil conservation hearings."],
["1935","The Soil Conservation Service is created. Federal programmes pay for contour farming, reseeding and land retirement."],
["1930s","About 2.5 million people leave the plains states. John Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath, 1939, fixes the exodus in national memory."],
["Late 1930s to 1940s","Rain returns, conservation practices spread, and the plains stabilise, though marginal land farming recurs in later wet decades."],
["Today","The Ogallala aquifer now plays the grassland's role in a slower sequel, mined for irrigation, depletion measured and largely deferred."]]},
{"id":"great-smog-london-1952","name":"The Great Smog of London, 1952","year":1952,"country":"United Kingdom","industry":"Energy, air quality and public health","founded":1952,"failed":1952,
"summary":"From 5 to 9 December 1952, a cold anticyclone settled over London and trapped the smoke of a coal burning city under an inversion layer. The result was a yellow black fog so dense that visibility fell to metres, transport stopped, a performance at Sadler's Wells was abandoned because the audience could not see the stage, and cattle at Smithfield market suffocated. Londoners, accustomed to pea soupers, initially treated it as weather. The death registers disagreed: about 4,000 excess deaths were recorded in the immediate weeks, and later research raised the toll to roughly 12,000, with perhaps 100,000 people made ill, the elderly, infants and those with heart and lung disease dying at home and in overwhelmed hospitals. Coal was not an industry choice but a household one: millions of domestic fires burning cheap, sulphurous coal, defended as the poor man's warmth against smokeless fuel prices. The government first blamed influenza. Within four years, however, the disaster produced the Clean Air Act of 1956, smoke control areas and the conversion of cities to smokeless fuels and gas, one of the most successful environmental laws of the century, passed because the evidence had been delivered, fatally, to every doorstep in the capital at once.",
"mistakes":"The disaster was a slow subsidy reaching its invoice: cheap coal's real price had been externalised into lungs for a century, and the fog merely concentrated the arithmetic into five visible days. Officialdom's first instinct was narrative management, an influenza epidemic, rather than the measurement sitting in its own registrars' books. Household behaviour was then blamed where infrastructure was the cage: families burned what was cheap because clean fuel cost more, a pricing decision made far above the fireplace. And London had been warned by centuries of fogs and a 1948 disaster in Donora, Pennsylvania, that killed 20: the knowledge existed; only the coalition to act on it was missing, until the smog built one out of mourners.",
"lessons":"Externalities do not vanish; they accumulate until they become weather. Price the pollution or the lungs settle the account, and they settle it regressively, on the old, the young and the poor first. When the official story and the death register disagree, believe the register and ask who benefits from the story. Clean air in London was not achieved by exhorting households but by changing what fuel cost and what chimneys were legal: infrastructure and prices, not sermons, clear skies. Finally, disasters that touch everyone at once legislate fastest; the task of policy in ordinary times is to act without requiring one.",
"timeline":[
["19th century onward","London's coal fogs are a cultural institution and a chronic killer, recorded in literature and mortality tables alike."],
["Oct 1948","A lethal smog in Donora, Pennsylvania, kills 20 and sickens thousands, an ignored rehearsal across the Atlantic."],
["5 to 9 Dec 1952","The Great Smog: five days of near zero visibility. Hospitals overflow; undertakers run short of coffins."],
["Dec 1952 to 1953","Excess deaths are first estimated around 4,000, then revised upward. The government initially resists a coal explanation."],
["1954","The Beaver Committee reports on air pollution, recommending clean air legislation and smoke control."],
["1956","The Clean Air Act passes: smoke control areas, grants for converting fireplaces, and controls on industrial emissions."],
["1960s","London's killer fogs end as smokeless fuels, gas and later North Sea gas replace household coal."],
["Today","Later studies put the Great Smog's toll near 12,000 deaths. London's air battles have moved to nitrogen dioxide and particulates, same argument, smaller particles."]]},
{"id":"minamata-disease","name":"Minamata Disease: The Cat That Danced and the Company That Waited","year":1956,"country":"Japan","industry":"Chemicals and environmental health","founded":1932,"failed":1956,
"summary":"From 1932 the Chisso chemical factory in Minamata, Japan, discharged wastewater containing methylmercury, a byproduct of acetaldehyde production, into Minamata Bay. The mercury concentrated up the food chain into the fish and shellfish that were the fishing town's diet and livelihood. By the early 1950s cats were convulsing and falling into the sea, crows dropped from the sky, and fishing families began developing numbness, constricted vision, slurred speech and convulsions. The disease was officially recognised in May 1956. Chisso's own doctor proved the cause by 1959, feeding factory effluent to a cat that developed the disease; the company suppressed the result, negotiated token payments with victims that waived future claims, and continued discharging, even rerouting waste to a river mouth that spread the poisoning to new villages. The state protected the company town's economics for over a decade; the government formally acknowledged the cause only in 1968, the year production ended. Thousands were certified as patients after gruelling fights; thousands more were refused. Compensation litigation ran for half a century, and Minamata became the world word for industrial poisoning, lending its name to the 2013 global mercury treaty.",
"mistakes":"Chisso committed the full sequence this graveyard exists to document: cause the harm, prove it internally, bury the proof, buy silence cheaply with sympathy payments structured as waivers, and keep polluting while negotiating. The town's dependence on the factory as employer was wielded as a shield, splitting victims from neighbours. The national government, prioritising industrial growth, delayed acknowledgment for nine years after the internal proof existed. Certification then became a second gatekeeping, recognising a fraction of the poisoned and forcing the rest into decades of litigation. Every institution optimised for the polluter's continuity and called the optimisation realism.",
"lessons":"Internal proof suppressed is the moment negligence becomes something worse; companies should treat their own scientists' unwelcome results as the fire alarm, not the fire. Sympathy money with waivers is not compassion, it is evidence management, and courts and history eventually price it as such. A town that cannot afford to close its poisoner has already paid; the only question is the currency, and mercury collects in children. And the state's delay compounded every private failure: when government finally spoke in 1968 it confirmed what the cats had announced in 1953. Believe the cats early.",
"timeline":[
["1932","Chisso begins acetaldehyde production in Minamata, discharging mercury laden wastewater into the bay."],
["Early 1950s","Cats convulse and drown themselves, fish float, crows fall. Fishing families report strange neurological symptoms."],
["1 May 1956","Minamata disease is officially reported after children are hospitalised with an unknown brain illness."],
["1959","University researchers and Chisso's own factory doctor identify organic mercury from effluent as the cause. Chisso suppresses its cat experiment and signs token solatium agreements with waivers."],
["1959 onward","Waste is rerouted to the Minamata River mouth, spreading contamination. Protests, sit ins and negotiations continue through the 1960s."],
["1968","The Japanese government formally acknowledges methylmercury from Chisso as the cause. Acetaldehyde production ends the same year."],
["1973","Kumamoto District Court rules for the plaintiffs, establishing full compensation and the template for pollution litigation in Japan."],
["2013","The Minamata Convention on Mercury, the global treaty to curb mercury pollution, is adopted and named for the town."]]},
{"id":"takata-airbags","name":"Takata and the Airbags That Became Shrapnel","year":2017,"country":"Japan","industry":"Automotive safety and manufacturing","founded":1933,"failed":2017,
"summary":"Takata, founded in 1933, grew into one of the world's largest makers of seatbelts and airbags, supplying most major car manufacturers. Its airbag inflators used ammonium nitrate propellant without a proper drying agent, a chemistry that can degrade in heat and humidity until the inflator, instead of inflating the bag in a controlled burn, explodes and fires metal fragments into the cabin. By the time the defect was undeniable, tens of millions of vehicles were recalled worldwide, eventually around 100 million inflators, the largest automotive safety recall in history. At least two dozen deaths and hundreds of injuries across manufacturers were linked to rupturing inflators, often in minor collisions that occupants should have walked away from. Investigations found that Takata had manipulated and misrepresented test data, that warnings inside the company went unheeded for years, and that car makers had accepted the cheaper propellant long after rivals considered it too unstable. Takata pleaded guilty to criminal wire fraud in the United States in 2017, agreed to a billion dollar resolution, and filed for bankruptcy that June, its viable divisions sold to a rival. Airbag recalls were still being completed a decade after the crisis broke, because the defective parts had been installed, by then, almost everywhere.",
"mistakes":"The chemistry was the original sin: choosing an unstable propellant to win cost contracts, in a device whose only job is to be safe on the worst day of a car's life, demanded perfect manufacturing forever. When degradation appeared, the company's response chose narrative over engineering, disputing root causes and misreporting tests while the fleet carrying the risk grew every model year. Secrecy compounded across the supply chain: car makers treated supplier quality as paperwork, and regulators learned the defect's scope from lawsuits and journalism more than from the industry's own telemetry. Each year of managed disclosure added millions of vehicles to the largest recall ever run.",
"lessons":"Never save money inside a safety device with a chemistry that has a known failure mode; the recall will collect the savings with interest denominated in funerals. Treat internal test anomalies as the product speaking, because a decade later prosecutors will read the same memos aloud. Disclosure speed is a design parameter: Takata's slow drip converted a defect into a fraud case and a bankruptcy. And when your component sits inside everyone else's product, your quality system is a public utility; Takata's failure closed factories and recall workshops on four continents. Audit accordingly.",
"timeline":[
["1990s to 2000s","Takata adopts ammonium nitrate inflators without desiccant and wins major contracts on cost."],
["2000s","Field ruptures occur. Internal concerns and manipulated test reports later become central to investigations."],
["2008","First limited recalls of Honda vehicles for rupturing inflators begin, small batches that understate the scope."],
["2013 to 2014","Recalls widen across manufacturers. A New York Times investigation exposes years of secret testing and warnings."],
["2015","United States regulators impose record civil penalties and oversight; Takata admits the defect at scale."],
["Jan 2017","Takata pleads guilty to wire fraud in the United States and agrees to about 1 billion dollars in fines and restitution."],
["Jun 2017","Takata files for bankruptcy protection in Japan and the United States; its sound businesses are sold to Key Safety Systems."],
["2020s","Recall repairs pass 100 million inflators globally and continue for years, the tail of a decision made for pennies per unit."]]}
]
